2,2679,400,64,3,323,400,65,3,525,1931,5109,11,400,64,61,17,65,61,18,34433,37018,90,18,17,15170,17,22,31716,11,323,57960,37018,90,64,15170,65,61,18,92,34433,37018,90,17,22,15170,19,31716,11,1128,374,400,64,35093,3,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIf $a$ and $b$ are real numbers, $a^2b^3=\\frac{32}{27}$, and $\\frac{a}{b^3}=\\frac{27}{4}$, what is $a+b$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"42415796025dd25f6e58cd12e5d2a228f91c72d5c5ddaabc597cc0ee83589759"},"seconds":26.241207122802734,"seed":295861257447587859,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for \\(a + b\\) given the equations \\(a^2b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4}\\), we will proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the second equation in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b^3} = \\frac{27}{4} \\implies a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\) into the first equation \\(a^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} b^3 \\right)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{27}{4} \\right)^2 (b^3)^2 b^3 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{729}{16} \\right) b^6 = \\frac{32}{27}\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(b^6\\), multiply both sides by \\(\\frac{16}{729}\\):\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32}{27} \\cdot \\frac{16}{729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{32 \\cdot 16}{27 \\cdot 729}\n\\]\n\\[\nb^6 = \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\\[\nb^6 = \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the sixth root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nb = \\pm \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(b = \\frac{8}{27}\\) back into \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( \\frac{8}{27} \\right)^3\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot \\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27 \\cdot 512}{4 \\cdot 19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{13824}{78732}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{512}{2916}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{128}{729}\n\\]\n\nThus, one solution is \\(a = \\frac{128}{729}\\) and \\(b = \\frac{8}{27}\\).\n\nNow, substitute \\(b = -\\frac{8}{27}\\) back into \\(a = \\frac{27}{4} b^3\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\left( -\\frac{8}{27} \\right)^3\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{27}{4} \\cdot -\\frac{512}{19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = -\\frac{27 \\cdot 512}{4 \\cdot 19683}\n\\]\n\\[\na = -\\frac{13824}{78732}\n\\]\n\\[\na = -\\frac{512}{2916}\n\\]\n\\[\na = -\\frac{128}{729}\n\\]\n\nThus, another solution is \\(a = -\\frac{128}{729}\\) and \\(b = -\\frac{8}{27}\\).\n\nFinally, calculate \\(a + b\\) for both solutions:\n1. \\(a + b = \\frac{128}{729} + \\frac{8}{27} = \\frac{128}{729} + \\frac{216}{729} = \\frac{344}{729}\\)\n2. \\(a + b = -\\frac{128}{729} - \\frac{8}{27} = -\\frac{128}{729} - \\frac{216}{729} = -\\frac{344}{729}\\)\n\nSince the problem does not specify which solution to choose, we present both:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{344}{729}} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad \\boxed{-\\frac{344}{729}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-\\frac{344}{729}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:912"}
